==============================================================================
1. Repeated values of D and of |D| among cells 1 <= s < k <= 400 (their range: k <= 60)
==============================================================================
signed D attained twice: {5: [(3, 1), (5, 3)], 13: [(4, 1), (8, 5)]}
|D| attained twice     : {1: [(2, 1, 1), (3, 2, -1)], 5: [(3, 1, 1), (5, 3, 1)], 13: [(4, 1, 1), (8, 5, 1)]}
  -> signed: D = 5, 13.  In absolute value ALSO |D| = 1 ((2,1) with D = +1 and (3,2) with D = -1).

  Pillai / Stroeker-Tijdeman is a statement about the SIGNED equation 3^x - 2^y = c.  With
  absolute values and no restriction x < y it is false for c > 13:
     |3^x - 2^y| = 1: (x, y, signed value) = [(0, 1, -1), (1, 1, 1), (1, 2, -1), (2, 3, 1)]
     |3^x - 2^y| = 5: (x, y, signed value) = [(1, 3, -5), (2, 2, 5), (3, 5, -5)]
     |3^x - 2^y| = 7: (x, y, signed value) = [(0, 3, -7), (2, 1, 7), (2, 4, -7)]
     |3^x - 2^y| = 13: (x, y, signed value) = [(1, 4, -13), (5, 8, -13)]
     |3^x - 2^y| = 23: (x, y, signed value) = [(2, 5, -23), (3, 2, 23)]
  e.g. 3^3 - 2^2 = 23 = 2^5 - 3^2.  Inside Collatz cells (s < k) the second sign never occurs for
  c > 1, which is why the researcher's table is right although the citation was mis-stated.

