4. Is '3x+5 and 3x+13 are cycle-rich BECAUSE 5 and 13 are the repeated denominators' right?
   Free cycles of 3x+c (cells with |D| = c, gcd(C, D) = 1), k <= 24, by c:
==============================================================================
   c =  139:  29 free cycles in cells {(11, 7): 29}
   c =   13:   8 free cycles in cells {(4, 1): 1, (8, 5): 7}
   c =  101:   5 free cycles in cells {(7, 3): 5}
   c =  175:   5 free cycles in cells {(8, 4): 5}
   c =   47:   5 free cycles in cells {(7, 4): 5}
   c =   37:   3 free cycles in cells {(6, 3): 3}
   c =    5:   3 free cycles in cells {(3, 1): 1, (5, 3): 2}
   c =  115:   3 free cycles in cells {(7, 5): 3}
   c =  119:   2 free cycles in cells {(7, 2): 2}
   c =   17:   2 free cycles in cells {(6, 4): 2}
   c =   23:   2 free cycles in cells {(5, 2): 2}
   c =   55:   1 free cycles in cells {(6, 2): 1}
   c =  125:   1 free cycles in cells {(7, 1): 1}
   c =   61:   1 free cycles in cells {(6, 1): 1}
   -> 3x+13 owes 7 of its 8 free cycles to the single cell (8,5); 3x+5 owes 2 of 3 to (5,3).
      3x+37 has 3 and 3x+47 has 5 free cycles from ONE cell each; 3x+139 has 29.  The number of
      free cycles is the necklace count of the cell(s), which is large when |D| is small for the
      size of the cell (record cells).  The repetition of D adds exactly one cycle (the cells
      (3,1) and (4,1) have one necklace).  So the README's causal sentence is an overstatement.

