5. Multiples of unit cells.  Doubled: D(2k,2s)^2 = 8 T_n + 1.  Tripled: |D(3k,3s)| = 6 T_n + 1
   (centered hexagonal number), where T_n = 2^(k-1) 3^s is the cell's triangular number.
==============================================================================
   cell (1,0): T_1 = 1;  D(2k,2s) = 3, D^2 = 9 = 8*1+1;  D(3k,3s) = 7, |D| = 6*1+1
   cell (1,1): T_2 = 3;  D(2k,2s) = -5, D^2 = 25 = 8*3+1;  D(3k,3s) = -19, |D| = 6*3+1
   cell (2,1): T_3 = 6;  D(2k,2s) = 7, D^2 = 49 = 8*6+1;  D(3k,3s) = 37, |D| = 6*6+1
   cell (3,2): T_8 = 36;  D(2k,2s) = -17, D^2 = 289 = 8*36+1;  D(3k,3s) = -217, |D| = 6*36+1
   -> 37 = 2^6 - 3^3 = 6*T_3 + 1 comes from the cell (2,1), whose triangular number is 6;
      the '36' in 37 = 36 + 1 is 6*T_3, not T_8.  The cell (3,2) that owns 36 = T_8 gives
      17 = sqrt(8*36 + 1) and 217 = 6*36 + 1 instead.  That 6*T_3 = T_8 is the coincidence
      6 * 6 = 36; no further mechanism was found.  Control: the same recipe gives 7 = 6*1+1
      and 19 = 6*3+1 for the other two unit cells.

