7. NEW (skeptic): the lattice point (2,2) itself lies on the -17 cycle.
   v = n + 1; a run of odd steps starting at v = lam*2^a is lam*2^a, lam*2^(a-1)*3, ..., lam*3^a.
==============================================================================
   cycle of 1: n = [1, 2]
        v = n+1 = [2, 3]
        |v| = lam * 2^a * 3^b, (a, b, lam): [(1, 0, 1), (0, 1, 1)]
   cycle of -5: n = [-5, -7, -10]
        v = n+1 = [-4, -6, -9]
        |v| = lam * 2^a * 3^b, (a, b, lam): [(2, 0, 1), (1, 1, 1), (0, 2, 1)]
   cycle of -17: n = [-17, -25, -37, -55, -82, -41, -61, -91, -136, -68, -34]
        v = n+1 = [-16, -24, -36, -54, -81, -40, -60, -90, -135, -67, -33]
        |v| = lam * 2^a * 3^b, (a, b, lam): [(4, 0, 1), (3, 1, 1), (2, 2, 1), (1, 3, 1), (0, 4, 1), (3, 0, 5), (2, 1, 5), (1, 2, 5), (0, 3, 5), (0, 0, 67), (0, 1, 11)]
   -> first circuit of the -17 cycle: v = -(2^4, 2^3*3, 2^2*3^2, 2*3^3, 3^4): the whole
      anti-diagonal a + b = 4 with lam = -1, passing through -(2^2 * 3^2) = -36 at n = -37.
      The three non-trivial known cycles contain unit-lam runs of height 1 ({1,2}), 2 (-5)
      and 4 (-17), because their minima are 2 - 1, -(2^2 + 1), -(2^4 + 1).
      Exits of those runs are consecutive pairs (v_top, 2v'): (3,4), (8,9), (80,81).
